  "find_out": {
    "windows": ["Press Ctrl + Shift + Esc to open Task Manager.", "Click Performance (the second icon down on the left).", "Click Memory: the big number at the top right is your RAM, for example \"16.0 GB\".", "Click GPU 0 (and GPU 1, if there is one). The name is at the top right. \"Dedicated GPU memory\" is the graphics card's own memory. If it says 0.5 GB or less, you have no separate graphics card."],
    "macos": ["Click the Apple menu in the top-left corner of the screen.", "Choose About This Mac.", "\"Chip\" is the processor (for example Apple M2), and \"Memory\" is the number we need (for example 16 GB). On a Mac, the graphics share this memory, so that's all there is to find."],
    "linux": ["Open a terminal.", "Type free -h and press Enter. The number under \"total\" on the Mem line is your RAM.", "Type lspci | grep -i vga and press Enter to see your graphics card. With an NVIDIA card, nvidia-smi shows its memory."],
    "steamos": ["Every Steam Deck has 16 GB of memory, so pick the LCD or OLED model and you're done.", "Not sure which one you have? The OLED has an orange power button. The LCD's is grey."]
